{
  "response": "# Accessibility Design Critic Review: Search Results with Live Region\n\n## Phase 0 \u2014 Test Evidence\n\nNo automated test results (axe-core, Pa11y, Playwright keyboard tests, or virtual-screen-reader logs) were provided. Findings below are based on source-code analysis. Several claims would be strengthened by a measured pass \u2014 noted where relevant.\n\n---\n\n## Phase 1 \u2014 Pre-commitment Predictions\n\nBefore reading the code, for a **search form + live region + dynamic results** component, I predicted:\n\n| # | Prediction | Likelihood |\n|---|-----------|-----------|\n| P1 | The `<label>` is hidden with `display: none` (a common \"I'll handle it with aria-label\" shortcut) | High |\n| P2 | Redundant or conflicting accessible name (label + aria-label + placeholder) | High |\n| P3 | Live region content changes are correct, but `aria-busy` is missing on the results container | Medium |\n| P4 | The \"no results\" live-region message drops context the visual message includes | Medium |\n| P5 | Results items are not actionable (no links) \u2014 either intentional or a gap | Medium |\n\nI will verify each against the source.\n\n---\n\n## Phase 2 \u2014 Semantic HTML Audit\n\n### 2a. Label hidden with `display: none` \u2014 **MAJOR**\n\n```css\n/* CSS, line: .search-container label */\n.search-container label {\n  display: none; /* Hidden but accessible */\n}\n```\n\n**Evidence:** The `<label htmlFor=\"search-input\">Search</label>` in the JSX is rendered, but the CSS applies `display: none`. The inline comment *\"Hidden but accessible\"* is **factually incorrect**. `display: none` removes the element from both the visual rendering **and** the accessibility tree. It is not a visually-hidden technique.\n\nThe component already defines a correct `.sr-only` class (clip-based, element remains in the a11y tree) but does **not** apply it to the label.\n\n**Impact:**\n- **Sighted users:** No visible label. The only text near the input is the `placeholder=\"Search for...\"`, which disappears on focus and is not a label substitute (WCAG 3.3.2 *Labels or Instructions*).\n- **Screen reader users:** The `aria-label=\"Search query\"` on the input provides an accessible name, so the input is *not* unnamed. However, the `<label>` element is dead code \u2014 its text \"Search\" is never used because `aria-label` takes precedence in the accessible-name computation (WCAG 4.1.2).\n- **Future developers:** The misleading comment will cause the wrong pattern to be copied elsewhere.\n\n**Fix:**\n```jsx\n// Remove aria-label from the input. Use the sr-only class on the label.\n<label htmlFor=\"search-input\" className=\"sr-only\">Search</label>\n<input\n  id=\"search-input\"\n  type=\"text\"\n  /* remove aria-label=\"Search query\" */\n  ...\n/>\n```\nOr, if a visible label is desired, remove `display: none` from the CSS and style the label normally.\n\n**WCAG:** 3.3.2 (Labels or Instructions), 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value)\n**Confidence:** HIGH \u2014 the CSS rule is unambiguous.\n\n---\n\n### 2b. Redundant / conflicting accessible name \u2014 **MINOR**\n\nThree sources of name for the same input:\n\n| Source | Value | Precedence |\n|--------|-------|------------|\n| `aria-label=\"Search query\"` | \"Search query\" | **Wins** (highest) |\n| `<label>Search</label>` | \"Search\" | Second (but `display:none` removes it) |\n| `placeholder=\"Search for...\"` | \"Search for...\" | Lowest (not a name source) |\n\nThe effective accessible name is **\"Search query\"**. The label text \"Search\" is never used. The placeholder is a hint, not a name. Three different strings for one concept is a maintenance hazard.\n\n**Fix:** Pick one. If the label is the source of truth, remove `aria-label`. If `aria-label` is the source of truth, remove the `<label>` element entirely (or keep it visible and remove `aria-label`).\n\n**WCAG:** 4.1.2\n**Confidence:** HIGH\n\n---\n\n### 2c. Form lacks a landmark role \u2014 **ENHANCEMENT**\n\n```jsx\n<form onSubmit={handleSearch}>\n```\n\nNo `role=\"search\"` and no `aria-label` on the `<form>`. Screen reader users navigating by landmarks (NVDA: `+L` for landmarks, VoiceOver: rotor \u2192 Landmarks) will not find a \"search\" landmark. The form is anonymous.\n\n**Fix:**\n```jsx\n<form role=\"search\" aria-label=\"Site search\" onSubmit={handleSearch}>\n```\n\n**WCAG:** 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships)\n**Confidence:** HIGH\n\n---\n\n### 2d. Heading hierarchy \u2014 **OK**\n\n`<h2>Search Results</h2>` \u2192 `<h3>{result.title}</h3>` is a valid h2\u2192h3 step. No skips. Assumes the page provides an `<h1>`. \u2713\n\n### 2e. List semantics \u2014 **OK**\n\n`<ul>` \u2192 `<li>` for results. Correct. \u2713\n\n### 2f. Native HTML usage \u2014 **OK**\n\n`<form>`, `<label>`, `<input>`, `<button>`, `<ul>`, `<li>`, `<h2>`, `<h3>`, `<p>` \u2014 all native. No `div role=\"button\"` anti-patterns. \u2713\n\n---\n\n## Phase 3 \u2014 ARIA Pattern Compliance Audit\n\n### 3a. Live region pattern \u2014 **CORRECT, with one redundancy**\n\n```jsx\n<div\n  role=\"status\"\n  aria-live=\"polite\"\n  aria-atomic=\"true\"\n  className=\"sr-only\"\n>\n```\n\n| Check | Status |\n|-------|--------|\n| `role=\"status\"` present | \u2713 |\n| `aria-live=\"polite\"` present | \u2713 (redundant \u2014 `role=\"status\"` already implies `aria-live=\"polite\"`) |\n| `aria-atomic=\"true\"` present | \u2713 (ensures full message announced as one unit) |\n| Element persists in DOM (not conditionally mounted) | \u2713 \u2014 the `<div>` is always rendered; only its text content changes |\n| Content changes trigger announcement | \u2713 \u2014 React re-render changes text nodes, browser live-region mechanism fires |\n| `sr-only` class keeps it in a11y tree | \u2713 \u2014 clip-based, not `display:none` |\n\nThe pattern matches the WAI-ARIA APG *Live Regions* guidance. The explicit `aria-live=\"polite\"` is redundant with `role=\"status\"` but not harmful.\n\n**Verdict:** The live region implementation is **sound**. No pattern gap.\n\n### 3b. No other ARIA patterns required\n\nThe component has no tabs, menus, dialogs, comboboxes, or composite widgets. The form is a standard HTML form. No ARIA pattern violations.\n\n---\n\n## Phase 4 \u2014 Focus Management Review\n\n### 4a. Tab order \u2014 **OK**\n\nLogical flow: `input` \u2192 `button` \u2192 (results, if present). No skipped elements, no z-index traps. \u2713\n\n### 4b. Focus after search submission \u2014 **ACCEPTABLE, noted**\n\nAfter pressing Enter or clicking \"Search\", focus remains on the submit button. When results appear, focus does **not** move to the results heading. The live region announces \"Found 3 results\", so screen reader users are informed. Keyboard users can Tab into the results.\n\nThis is a defensible design choice (the user may want to refine the query). However, if the results are the primary content, moving focus to the `<h2>` would be more discoverable:\n\n```js\n// Optional enhancement\nconst resultsHeadingRef = useRef(null);\n// After setResults(...):\nsetTimeout(() => resultsHeadingRef.current?.focus(), 0);\n```\n\n**Severity:** ENHANCEMENT (not a violation; the live region compensates)\n**WCAG:** 2.4.3 (Focus Order) \u2014 current order is logical, just not optimal\n**Confidence:** HIGH\n\n### 4c. No keyboard traps \u2014 **OK**\n\nNo modals, no focus traps, no `tabindex` manipulation. \u2713\n\n### 4d. No skip link \u2014 **N/A (component-level)**\n\nThis is a component, not a page. The host page should provide a skip link. Not a finding here.\n\n### 4e. No SPA route-change focus management \u2014 **N/A**\n\nThis is a search-within-page component, not a route change. \u2713\n\n---\n\n## Phase 5 \u2014 State Communication Audit\n\n### 5a. Loading state \u2014 **MOSTLY OK, one gap**\n\n| Channel | Present? | Detail |\n|---------|----------|--------|\n| Visual indicator | \u2713 | `<p className=\"loading\">Searching...</p>` |\n| Live region announcement | \u2713 | \"Searching...\" in `role=\"status\"` |\n| `aria-busy=\"true\"` on results container | \u2717 | Missing |\n\nThe live region handles the announcement, so the absence of `aria-busy` is mitigated. However, `aria-busy` on the results container would tell screen readers \"this region is being updated, don't announce intermediate content.\" Without it, a screen reader user who is navigating the results list when a new search starts may hear the old results being removed before the new ones appear.\n\n**Fix:**\n```jsx\n<div className=\"results-container\" aria-busy={isLoading}>\n```\n\n**Severity:** MINOR (live region compensates)\n**WCAG:** 4.1.3 (Status Messages)\n**Confidence:** MEDIUM\n\n### 5b. Results-announced state \u2014 **OK**\n\n\"Found 3 results\" is announced via the live region. The results are in the DOM with semantic structure. \u2713\n\n### 5c. No-results state \u2014 **MINOR inconsistency**\n\n| Channel | Message |\n|---------|---------|\n| Visual | `No results found for \"quantum physics\"` |\n| Live region | `No results found` |\n\nThe screen reader user does **not** hear the query. They know there are no results but lose the context of *what* was searched. This is a small but real information gap.\n\n**Fix:**\n```jsx\n{!isLoading && results.length === 0 && query && `No results found for \"${query}\"`}\n```\n\n**Severity:** MINOR\n**WCAG:** 4.1.3 (Status Messages)\n**Confidence:** HIGH\n\n### 5d. Disabled / readonly states \u2014 **N/A**\n\nNo disabled or readonly elements in this component. \u2713\n\n### 5e. Visual-only indicators \u2014 **OK**\n\nAll state changes (loading, results, no-results) are communicated both visually and programmatically. No color-only indicators. \u2713\n\n### 5f. Potential double-announcement \u2014 **MINOR**\n\nThe visual loading message `<p className=\"loading\">Searching...</p>` is in the normal DOM flow. A screen reader user navigating the page content will encounter this text. Simultaneously, the live region announces \"Searching...\". In practice, the live region announcement is automatic and the visual text is just there for sighted users, so the double-announcement is unlikely to cause confusion. But it is a minor redundancy.\n\n**Fix (optional):** Add `aria-hidden=\"true\"` to the visual loading `<p>` since the live region already announces the state:\n```jsx\n{isLoading && <p className=\"loading\" aria-hidden=\"true\">Searching...</p>}\n```\n\n**Severity:** MINOR\n**Confidence:** MEDIUM\n\n---\n\n## Phase 6 \u2014 Multi-Perspective Review\n\n### Screen reader user (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver)\n\n- **Form:** Input has accessible name \"Search query\" (from `aria-label`). Functional, but the `<label>` is dead code. The form has no landmark role.\n- **Live region:** Correctly announces \"Searching\u2026\", \"Found 3 results\", \"No results found\". The `sr-only` class is properly implemented.\n- **Results:** Semantic `<ul>` \u2192 `<li>` with `<h3>` headings. Navigable by heading or list.\n- **Gap:** \"No results\" message lacks the query context.\n- **Gap:** No `role=\"search\"` landmark.\n- **Overall:** Functional. The live region pattern is the strongest part of this component.\n\n### Keyboard-only user\n\n- Tab order: input \u2192 button \u2192 results. Logical.\n- No keyboard traps.\n- No undiscoverable shortcuts.\n- Focus stays on button after submit. Acceptable.\n- **Overall:** No issues.\n\n### Low vision user (200% zoom, high contrast)\n\n- **Contrast ratios (calculated):**\n\n| Element | Foreground | Background | Ratio | Required | Pass? |\n|---------|-----------|------------|-------|----------|-------|\n| Button text | `#FFFFFF` | `#0066CC` | **5.57:1** | 4.5:1 (normal text) | \u2713 |\n| Input border | `#0066CC` | `#FFFFFF` | **5.57:1** | 3:1 (UI component) | \u2713 |\n| h2 \"Search Results\" | `#333333` | `#FFFFFF` | **12.6:1** | 4.5:1 | \u2713 |\n| h3 result titles | `#0066CC` | `#FFFFFF` | **5.57:1** | 4.5:1 | \u2713 |\n| Description text | `#666666` | `#FFFFFF` | **5.74:1** | 4.5:1 | \u2713 |\n| Loading text | `#0066CC` | `#FFFFFF` | **5.57:1** | 4.5:1 | \u2713 |\n| No-results text | `#666666` | `#FFFFFF` | **5.74:1** | 4.5:1 | \u2713 |\n\nAll pass. \u2713\n\n- **Touch targets:** Button \u2248 36px+ height, input \u2248 36px+ height. Both exceed 24\u00d724 CSS px minimum (WCAG 2.5.8). \u2713\n- **Reflow at 200%:** `max-width: 600px` with `flex` layout. Should reflow without horizontal scroll. \u2713\n- **High contrast mode:** Colors are explicit hex values, not `currentColor`. Should work. \u2713\n- **Overall:** No issues.\n\n### Cognitive accessibility\n\n- Clear, specific messages. \u2713\n- No timeouts. \u2713\n- No destructive actions. \u2713\n- Consistent interaction model (type \u2192 submit \u2192 results). \u2713\n- No results message is specific (\"No results found for X\"). \u2713\n- **Overall:** No issues.\n\n### Vestibular & motion\n\n- No animations, transitions, or auto-playing content in the CSS. \u2713\n- **Alarm: LOW**\n\n### Auditory access\n\n- No media elements. \u2713\n- **Alarm: LOW**\n\n### Environmental contrast\n\n- All contrast ratios verified above. \u2713\n- Color is never the sole indicator (text labels present). \u2713\n- **Alarm: LOW**\n\n### Perspective Alarm Table\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | **MEDIUM** | Live region present but label pattern is wrong; no search landmark |\n| Keyboard-only | **LOW** | Simple form, logical tab order, no traps |\n| Low vision | **LOW** | All contrast passes, targets sized, reflow OK |\n| Cognitive | **LOW** | Clear messages, consistent interaction |\n| Vestibular & motion | **LOW** | No animation or motion |\n| Auditory access | **LOW** | No media |\n| Environmental contrast | **LOW** | All ratios pass, no color-only indicators |\n\n---\n\n## Phase 7 \u2014 Gap Analysis (What's Missing)\n\n| # | Missing Item | Impact | Severity |\n|---|-------------|--------|----------|\n| G1 | Visible label for sighted users (label is `display:none`) | Sighted users rely on placeholder only | MAJOR |\n| G2 | `aria-busy` on results container during loading | Screen reader may announce stale content mid-update | MINOR |\n| G3 | Query context in \"no results\" live-region message | SR user loses what was searched | MINOR |\n| G4 | `role=\"search\"` or `aria-label` on `<form>` | No search landmark for SR navigation | ENHANCEMENT |\n| G5 | `autocomplete` attribute on input | Browser can't offer search history | ENHANCEMENT |\n| G6 | `aria-hidden=\"true\"` on visual loading text | Minor double-announcement risk | MINOR |\n| G7 | Results are not actionable (no `<a>` links) | If results should be clickable, they're not | OPEN QUESTION |\n| G8 | No guard against stale `setTimeout` callbacks | Rapid re-search could announce stale results | OPEN QUESTION |\n\n**Items checked and confirmed present:**\n- \u2713 Live region is persistent in DOM (not conditionally mounted)\n- \u2713 `sr-only` class is correctly implemented (clip-based)\n- \u2713 Semantic list structure for results\n- \u2713 Heading hierarchy (h2 \u2192 h3)\n- \u2713 Native HTML elements (no div-role anti-patterns)\n- \u2713 No color-only state indicators\n- \u2713 No `visibility: hidden` on focus-reveal elements\n- \u2713 No pseudo-element content in a11y tree\n- \u2713 No font-icon elements\n- \u2713 No `title` attribute used as sole accessible name\n- \u2713 No `role=\"presentation\"` on data tables\n- \u2713 No `aria-hidden` without `inert` on focusable content\n\n---\n\n## Phase 8 \u2014 Realist Check (Severity Calibration)\n\n| Finding | Initial Rating | Realist Check | Final Rating |\n|---------|---------------|---------------|--------------|\n| Label `display:none` | MAJOR | SR users get name via `aria-label`. Sighted users have placeholder. Workaround exists. But the pattern is wrong and the comment is misleading. | **MAJOR** (keep \u2014 wrong pattern + misleading comment will propagate) |\n| Redundant naming | MINOR | Functional. `aria-label` wins. No access loss. | **MINOR** (keep) |\n| No `aria-busy` | MINOR | Live region compensates. Edge case only. | **MINOR** (keep) |\n| No-results message | MINOR | SR user loses query context. Minor info loss. | **MINOR** (keep) |\n| No `role=\"search\"` | ENHANCEMENT | Nice-to-have. Not a barrier. | **ENHANCEMENT** (keep) |\n| Double-announcement | MINOR | Very unlikely to cause confusion in practice. | **MINOR** (keep, note as optional) |\n\nNo downgrades or upgrades needed. No findings involve complete access loss.\n\n---\n\n## Phase 9 \u2014 Self-Audit\n\n| Finding | Confidence | Could developer refute? | Gap or Preference? |\n|---------|-----------|------------------------|-------------------|\n| Label `display:none` | HIGH | No \u2014 CSS is unambiguous | **GAP** |\n| Redundant naming | HIGH | No \u2014 both attributes present in code | **GAP** (code quality) |\n| No `aria-busy` | MEDIUM | \"Live region handles it\" \u2014 valid mitigation | **GAP** (minor) |\n| No-results message | HIGH | No \u2014 strings are different in code | **GAP** (minor) |\n| No `role=\"search\"` | HIGH | No \u2014 not present | **ENHANCEMENT** |\n| Double-announcement | MEDIUM | \"SR users don't navigate that far\" \u2014 plausible | **PREFERENCE** (downgrade to optional note) |\n\nThe double-announcement finding is borderline preference. I'll keep it as MINOR but note it's optional.\n\nNo findings moved to Open Questions based on confidence. G7 and G8 are genuine open questions requiring developer context.\n\n---\n\n## Phase 10 \u2014 Synthesis vs. Predictions\n\n| Prediction | Outcome |\n|------------|---------|\n| P1: Label hidden with `display:none` | **CONFIRMED** \u2014 MAJOR finding |\n| P2: Redundant/conflicting name | **CONFIRMED** \u2014 MINOR finding |\n| P3: Missing `aria-busy` | **CONFIRMED** \u2014 MINOR finding |\n| P4: No-results message drops query | **CONFIRMED** \u2014 MINOR finding |\n| P5: Results not actionable | **OPEN QUESTION** \u2014 may be intentional |\n\nNo surprises. No predicted issues were missed. The live region implementation (my main uncertainty) turned out to be **correct and well-structured** \u2014 the persistent div, `aria-atomic`, and conditional content all work as intended.\n\n---\n\n## VERDICT: ACCEPT-WITH-RESERVATIONS\n\n**Overall Assessment:** The component's core accessibility architecture is sound. The live region pattern is correctly implemented (persistent DOM element, `role=\"status\"`, `aria-atomic`, conditional content), the semantic HTML is clean (native elements, proper list structure, valid heading hierarchy), and all contrast ratios pass. The primary concern is the label-hiding pattern (`display: none` with a misleading comment), which is a wrong pattern that will propagate if copied. The remaining findings are minor gaps in state communication completeness. No user is blocked from accessing or using this component.\n\n---\n\n### Major Findings (1)\n\n**M1. Label hidden with `display: none` \u2014 wrong pattern, misleading comment**\n- **Evidence:** CSS `.search-container label { display: none; }` with comment `/* Hidden but accessible */`. JSX: `<label htmlFor=\"search-input\">Search</label>` + `aria-label=\"Search query\"` on input.\n- **User group:** Sighted users (no visible label), all users (misleading code), future developers (pattern will be copied).\n- **WCAG:** 3.3.2 (Labels or Instructions), 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value)\n- **Why this matters:** `display: none` removes the label from the accessibility tree. The comment is factually wrong. Sighted users see only a placeholder. The `aria-label` compensates for screen readers, but the `<label>` is dead code and the pattern is incorrect.\n- **Fix:**\n  ```jsx\n  // Option A: Visible label (preferred)\n  <label htmlFor=\"search-input\">Search</label>\n  <input id=\"search-input\" type=\"text\" ... />  {/* remove aria-label */}\n  // Remove `display: none` from CSS\n\n  // Option B: Visually hidden label (if design requires no visible label)\n  <label htmlFor=\"search-input\" className=\"sr-only\">Search</label>\n  <input id=\"search-input\" type=\"text\" ... />  {/* remove aria-label */}\n  ```\n- **Confidence:** HIGH\n\n---\n\n### Minor Findings (4)\n\n**m1. Redundant/conflicting accessible name sources**\n- **Evidence:** `aria-label=\"Search query\"` (JSX line ~28), `<label>Search</label>` (JSX line ~25), `placeholder=\"Search for...\"` (JSX line ~31). Three different strings.\n- **WCAG:** 4.1.2\n- **Fix:** Use one source. If the label is the source of truth, remove `aria-label`. The placeholder is a hint, not a name.\n- **Confidence:** HIGH\n\n**m2. No `aria-busy` on results container during loading**\n- **Evidence:** `<div className=\"results-container\">` (JSX line ~49) has no `aria-busy` attribute. `isLoading` state exists but is not applied to this container.\n- **WCAG:** 4.1.3 (Status Messages)\n- **Why:** A screen reader user navigating the results list when a new search starts may hear old results being removed. `aria-busy` suppresses intermediate announcements.\n- **Fix:** `<div className=\"results-container\" aria-busy={isLoading}>`\n- **Confidence:** MEDIUM (live region compensates)\n\n**m3. \"No results\" live-region message omits the query**\n- **Evidence:** Live region: `'No results found'` (JSX line ~42). Visual: `No results found for \"{query}\"` (JSX line ~58).\n- **WCAG:** 4.1.3 (Status Messages)\n- **Fix:** `{!isLoading && results.length === 0 && query && \\`No results found for \"${query}\"\\`}`\n- **Confidence:** HIGH\n\n**m4. Visual loading text may cause minor double-announcement**\n- **Evidence:** `<p className=\"loading\">Searching...</p>` (JSX line ~45) is in normal DOM flow. Live region also announces \"Searching...\".\n- **WCAG:** 4.1.3\n- **Fix (optional):** Add `aria-hidden=\"true\"` to the visual `<p>` since the live region already announces the state.\n- **Confidence:** MEDIUM (low practical impact)\n\n---\n\n### Enhancements (2)\n\n- **e1.** Add `role=\"search\"` and `aria-label=\"Site search\"` to the `<form>` element for landmark navigation. (WCAG 1.3.1)\n- **e2.** Add `autocomplete=\"off\"` (or `\"search\"`) to the input. (WCAG 1.3.5)\n\n---\n\n### What's Missing\n\n- No visible label for sighted users (covered in M1)\n- No `aria-busy` on the results container (covered in m2)\n- No search landmark on the form (covered in e1)\n- No `autocomplete` attribute (covered in e2)\n- Results are not actionable \u2014 no `<a>` links. If results should be clickable, they need to be links with descriptive text. (Open question \u2014 may be intentional for this fixture)\n- No guard against stale `setTimeout` callbacks. Rapid re-search could cause the live region to announce results from a previous query. (Open question \u2014 logic bug more than a11y, but affects announcement accuracy)\n\n---\n\n### Multi-Perspective Notes\n\n- **Screen reader user:** The live region is the strongest part of this component \u2014 correctly implemented with persistent DOM, `aria-atomic`, and proper content changes. The main gap is the label pattern (M1) and the missing search landmark (e1). The \"no results\" message could include the query (m3).\n- **Keyboard-only user:** No issues. Logical tab order, no traps, no undiscoverable shortcuts. Focus stays on the button after submit, which is acceptable.\n- **Low vision user:** All contrast ratios pass (verified: 5.57:1 to 12.6:1). Touch targets exceed 24\u00d724 minimum. Layout reflows at 200% zoom. No issues.\n- **Cognitive accessibility:** Clear, specific messages. Consistent interaction model. No timeouts or destructive actions. No issues.\n\n---\n\n### Verdict Justification\n\n**ACCEPT-WITH-RESERVATIONS** rather than **ACCEPT** because:\n\n1. The `display: none` label pattern (M1) is a **wrong pattern with a misleading comment**. It works today because `aria-label` compensates, but it is not the correct technique, and the comment will cause the wrong pattern to be copied. This is a design decision that should be corrected before the pattern spreads.\n\n2. The remaining findings (m1\u2013m4, e1\u2013e2) are minor gaps that degrade the experience slightly but do not block access. They are quick fixes.\n\n**To upgrade to ACCEPT:** Fix M1 (use `sr-only` class or visible label, remove `aria-label`, fix the comment). The minor findings are recommended but not blocking.\n\n**What's working well (acknowledged, not manufactured):**\n- The live region is correctly implemented: persistent in DOM, `role=\"status\"`, `aria-atomic=\"true\"`, conditional content that changes to trigger announcements. This is the right pattern.\n- Semantic HTML is clean: native elements, proper list structure, valid heading hierarchy.\n- All contrast ratios pass.\n- The `sr-only` class is correctly implemented (clip-based, not `display:none`).\n- No ARIA anti-patterns (no div-role, no title-as-name, no role=presentation on data).\n\n---\n\n### Open Questions (unscored)\n\n1. **Are results meant to be actionable?** The `<li>` items contain `<h3>` and `<p>` but no links. If users should click a result to navigate, these need to be `<a>` elements with descriptive text. If they're display-only (e.g., a preview), the current structure is fine.\n2. **Stale search guard:** The `setTimeout` callback is not cancelled if the user submits a new search before the first completes. The second search's results would overwrite the first, but the first `setTimeout` would still fire and call `setResults` + `setIsLoading(false)`, potentially announcing stale results via the live region. Consider using an `AbortController` or a ref-based cancellation token.\n3. **Empty query submission:** `if (!query.trim()) return;` silently does nothing. No visual or programmatic feedback. Is this intentional? A screen reader user pressing Enter on an empty form gets no response.\n4. **Page context:** Does the host page provide an `<h1>`? The component starts at `<h2>`. If the page has no `<h1>`, the heading hierarchy starts at h2, which is a page-level issue, not a component issue.",
